/** Full translations for siman 086 — YD001 quality pass */
export const TRANSLATIONS = {
  'mechaber': {
    '1#main': `The signs of eggs and their laws. In it are 10 seifim: Signs of eggs to recognize that they are from a pure fowl — if both ends are rounded or pointed, or if the white was outside and the yolk inside — it is known that it is an egg of an impure fowl; and even if the seller says they are from a pure fowl, we do not rely on him. And if one end is round and spherical and the other end pointed and spherical, and the white is outside and the yolk inside — it is possible that it is an egg of an impure fowl and possible that it is an egg of a pure fowl; therefore one asks the hunter — a Jew (or a gentile) (Tur and Beit Yosef in name of Hagahot Maimoniyot and so too in name of R' Yitzchak ibn Chaviv) — the seller: if he told him it is from such-and-such fowl and we recognize that that fowl is pure — we rely on him; and if he said from a pure fowl and did not state its name — we do not rely on him; therefore one does not buy eggs from gentiles unless he recognized them and has in them a recognizable familiarity that they are eggs of such-and-such pure fowl; and we are not concerned lest they are eggs of a treifah or nevelah.`,
    '2#main': `Now the custom is to buy eggs indiscriminately from anyone, because eggs of impure fowl are not found among us: {Rama: And specifically with common eggs — meaning chicken eggs or goose eggs and the like; but one does not buy eggs from fowl that are not common more than from impure fowl until he sees the signs as explained (Arukh general rule 42).}`,
    '3#main': `An egg of nevelah and treifah — even if it was completed such that similar ones are sold in the market — is forbidden; and even if it was mixed into a thousand, all are forbidden; but a doubt whether it is an egg of nevelah or an egg of a slaughtered animal — it is permitted even without a mixture; but a doubt whether it is an egg of treifah or an egg of a slaughtered animal — it is forbidden: {Rama: And if the chicken was slaughtered and found treifah — the law of eggs is like the law of milk that was milked from an animal (Beit Yosef); and see above siman 81.}`,
    '4#main': `If one struck a chicken on its tail and it laid its egg before it was completed — it is permitted, and there is no concern here for eiver min ha'chai; provided it is not entangled in sinews.`,
    '5#main': `An egg of impure fowl that was cooked with others — if it is in its shell it does not forbid; and if it is peeled it forbids (and if it is pierced its law is like peeled) (Arukh general rule 42 and hagahah ShaDa); and one needs sixty-one permitted eggs corresponding to it to nullify it (and even if it is not whole but only broken) (in Arukh and Beit Yosef in name of Rashba); and if it was cooked in a permitted dish one needs only sixty of the dish to nullify it; and an egg that has a chick or blood forbids what is cooked with it even if it is not peeled: {Rama: And shells of prohibition combine also with permission to nullify the prohibition, and all the more shells of permission (Tosafot Nedarim 15 and Yerushalmi and Bavli — see s.k. 14); and see above siman 99.}`,
    '6#main': `An egg of treifah and nevelah — its law is like the law of an egg of impure fowl, that whatever is not peeled and was cooked with others does not forbid, for it is merely like water; {Rama: And some say all eggs have the same law and forbid even when not peeled (Tur in name of Beit HaTaharah and majority of poskim); and so one should practice where there is no great loss; but where there is great loss one may rely on the lenient.}`,
    '7#main': `A chick born from an egg of nevelah and treifah — permitted: {Rama: And some say we do not practice so (in Arukh); and l'chatchila one should be careful.}`,
    '8#main': `An egg of impure fowl in which the fowl began to form — they would flog because of sheretz of the fowl; and an egg of pure fowl in which the fowl began to form and she ate it — they would prepare for it a blow of rebelliousness.`,
    '9#main': `A treifah does not give birth; therefore a fowl that is a safek treifah and laid eggs — one waits with it until it lays all its eggs that it already claims; if it will lay again — it is kosher, and we know this when it paused between the end of one laying and the beginning of another a full twenty-one days, for such is its gestation period; and the same law for a chicken that never laid, born with a safek of treifot — that if it lays after twenty-one days from when it was born it is a safek treifah and the eggs are permitted (Orach Chaim brings it Beit Yosef end siman 57).`,
    '10#main': `One does not sell an egg of nevelah and treifah to a gentile lest he return and sell it to a Jew. How should he act? He breaks it in a bowl and sells it to a gentile — for then certainly a Jew will not buy it from him. In what case is this said — when we see it broken in his hand that there is concern a Jew sold it to him because it is treifah; but it is permitted to buy from him bread kneaded with eggs in a place where they are accustomed to eat bread of gentiles, since the broken egg is not seen in his hand: {Rama: And some say that now that we are not careful about bread of gentiles, one should not sell a forbidden egg to a gentile even broken, lest he mix it with bread and sell it to a Jew (Arukh general rule 42 and Mordechai in name of Semak); and so is the custom in a place where they make bread with eggs and practice leniency to buy it; but in other places there is no concern.}`,
  },
  'baer-heitev': {
    '1#א': `The seller. Shach wrote: even an Israelite established in kashrut who says they are from such-and-such fowl and we recognize that that fowl is pure — we do not rely on him; and even if he shows us the fowl and says the eggs come from it he is not believed, for he certainly lies.`,
    '1#ב': `Relies. The reason: when he says it is from such-and-such fowl it is a presumption he does not lie because he fears lest they bring eggs of that fowl and they see they do not resemble these and he is shamed; and Taz wrote there is no room to say that since there are raven eggs resembling dove eggs he could evade and say they are dove eggs; nevertheless he fears lest an Israelite set his mind on them that there cannot fail to be some difference between them.`,
    '1#ג': `Not. The reason: when they bring eggs of a pure fowl and they do not resemble them, he can say: I did not tell you these are from this fowl but from another fowl you do not recognize, and it is pure.`,
    '1#ד': `Tereifah. For most eggs are kosher; and Shach wrote that according to the Rav in his hagahah, even without recognizable familiarity we rely on him when he says they are from such-and-such fowl and we recognize that it is pure.`,
    '2#_': `In signs. Taz wrote: even if a non-Jewess brought and broke them before us unintentionally and we had not yet recognized by familiarity that they are chicken eggs — nevertheless permitted and we rely on the majority; except where the non-Jew explicitly says they are from an uncommon species — then signs are required. And Shach wrote in name of a sage that the custom is to buy bar-goose eggs called enten, some in white shells and some in green shells, because they recognize in them that they are bar-goose eggs whose way is thus.`,
    '3#א': `Nevelah. Shach wrote: an egg of nevelah is forbidden only d'rabbanan, for from the Torah it is permitted, for it is impossible it should be completed after death; but an egg of tereifah is forbidden from the Torah because afterward it was completed in prohibition, and even if it became tereifah only near shechitah it is forbidden from the Torah (they do not distinguish) — see above end of seif 99 and end of siman 87 seif 5.`,
    '3#ב': `In a thousand. This applies also to an egg of nevelah, for since it is an egg of nevelah the prohibition is certainly established and it is not nullified; unlike when it is a doubt of an egg of nevelah which is a rabbinic doubt and lenient. But in a doubt of an egg of tereifah, which is d'oraisa, they were stringent. However, it requires study according to what Mechaber and Rama ruled in siman 110 that it should nullify in a majority — dry in dry, one in two is nullified — and specifically an egg that has a chick in it is a complete creature, but an ordinary egg is not. And it requires study.`,
    '3#ג': `Permitted. For it is a rabbinic doubt; and even if we know certainly from which chicken it is, only that there is doubt whether the chicken is nevelah or slaughtered — such as doubt in shechitah where the halachah is it is invalid — the egg is permitted.`,
    '5#א': `Peeled. Shach wrote: it appears that even though the others are also in their shells it is forbidden until sixty-one, and the shell of the permitted combines — by way of example: if there are fifty permitted eggs and the shells hold ten eggs, that is insufficient but sixty-one are required (the reason is that peeled is the power of fire dominating it and extracting the body of the prohibition, and it is like an egg that has a chick in it which forbids even if not peeled — Taz, and Shach agreed). And not like Bach who is lenient if the forbidden is peeled and the permitted is not peeled and permits everything — this does not seem correct at all; see there.`,
    '5#ב': `Sixty-one. The reason: many poskim wrote that because there are large and small eggs they therefore added one; and for this reason when it was cooked in a permitted dish one needs only sixty of the dish to nullify it — this is Mechaber's view; but Rambam wrote the reason is because it is a complete creature in itself they made a distinction and added to its measure; and for this reason even in a dish one needs sixty-one to nullify; however for this reason if it became tereifah in a bowl one needs only sixty; and according to Mechaber one needs sixty-one — and one should be stringent for both reasons, end of his words.`,
    '5#ג': `Chick. The reason is that the meat is forbidden; and the same applies to blood found in it — a drop of blood that spreads through all of it, as above siman 66, and it is considered as if there is a chick in it — specifically when it is known the blood is in the place that forbids it; but in a doubt of mixtures it is permitted as in siman 66.`,
    '5#ד': `Siman 99. Meaning: there it was explained that where there is no great loss one may not combine bones of prohibition to nullify — and the same applies here; and Taz distinguishes that specifically in a dish the shell of prohibition combines with the permitted, but not with ordinary eggs — sixty-one are required to nullify it.`,
    '7#א': `Permitted. The reason is stated in the Gemara and poskim: because the chick is not formed from the egg until after it spoils, and it is like mere dust.`,
    '7#ב': `And some say. Shach wrote: and Taz omitted this entire hagahah and rightly intended — why should we be stringent in something found explicitly permitted in the Gemara and all poskim; and Taz wrote the reason one should be concerned lest he keep it in his house under the chicken to make a chick from it and it will be permitted, and there is concern for mishap lest meanwhile he eat it — as we find this matter stated explicitly in Mishnah chapter 8 of Terumot, that for this reason they decreed that growths of terumah too are terumah, lest he keep impure terumah until sowing time — and it requires study to resolve this in the Gemara, end of his words. And Nekudot HaKesef challenged this — some say for terumah which is common; see there.`,
    '8#_': `Rebelliousness. Perishah challenged: for in siman 66 Tur wrote that if it is known the chick is formed they are liable on it from the Torah; and he resolved: there, blood is found inside the egg — therefore forbidden from the Torah because of blood; unlike here where the blood already turned and became clotted and is dust and has no d'oraisa prohibition; and Taz in siman 66 resolved: here it speaks where there is no blood, only formation of flesh — and this is d'rabbanan, end of his words.`,
    '9#א': `We wait. Taz wrote: some say to what Rama wrote in siman 57 that we are concerned for mishap even in a doubt of tereifah — there it is different because one must wait twelve months; but here the time is short. But in Beit Yosef he wrote: even regarding fowl one does not wait except a day — immediately one slaughters and sells to a non-Jew, meaning sells to a non-Jew and the non-Jew slaughters afterward — see above siman 57.`,
    '9#ב': `Doubt. Shach wrote: but certainly tereifah — no; and see siman 57 and siman 81.`,
    '10#א': `In what case is this said. That it is forbidden to buy from a non-Jew.`,
    '10#ב': `They practice. And a sage wrote: nevertheless one should not buy cracked eggs from a non-Jew; see there. And Shach wrote: however, it may be that these poskim who permit speak of a non-Jew who is not signed; but a non-Jew who is commonly signed often mixes with bread — cracked eggs — and we are concerned lest he sell to an Israelite, end of his words.`,
  },
  'beer-hagolah': {
    '1#_': `There in the baraita.`,
    '2#_': `Conclusion of the Gemara there.`,
    '3#_': `Rambam there.`,
    '4#_': `Rosh there.`,
    '5#_': `Mishnah Beitzah.`,
    '6#_': `Tur, and in name of Beit HaTaharah, and like R' Yochanan who said: that which is sold by count we learned — egg on daf 3, Chullin daf 98; and see in siman 110 that he did not rule thus.`,
    '7#_': `There in the baraita.`,
    '8#_': `Rashba there.`,
    '9#_': `Rashba there.`,
    '10#_': `The statement of Rav Shemen bar Abba, Chullin daf 98; and the reason: because eggs are not equal in size — Ramban and Shach. (°) Beit Yosef wrote: therefore to nullify the taste one says that even if mixed in a thousand all are forbidden; and regarding an egg with a chick, it appears from his words that all of it is considered a complete creature and even in a thousand it is not nullified, etc.; and below in siman 98 seif 7 he ruled explicitly that one needs sixty-one to nullify its emission.`,
  },
  'beur-hagra': {
    '3#א': `Egg of nevelah, etc. — Chullin 64; Yerushalmi Terumot; Rambam — see there.`,
    '3#ב': `Egg of nevelah, etc. — Chullin 64; Yerushalmi Terumot; Rambam — see there.`,
    '4#_': `And it is, etc. Tosafot s.v. disgusting eggs cite Yerushalmi — disgusting eggs forbidden, but Ra'akva's version: disgusting eggs that formed are permitted — they are merely in the membrane and it is not skinned.`,
    '5#א': `Egg of, etc. There, 97, 98.`,
    '5#ב': `And if it is, etc. Tosafot there cite Yerushalmi and Ran; not like Ramban who permits even peeled — since our Gemara does not speak of peeled eggs.`,
    '5#ג': `And even. Rambam's reason: complete creature — therefore added one; if cracked, only sixty; but Rashba challenged on nullifying emission — see there.`,
    '5#ד': `And egg that has, etc. — see there.`,
    '5#ה': `Or blood. Blood spreads through all — like above siman 66; considered like a chick — see there.`,
    '5#ו': `And shells, etc. Yerushalmi Terumot chapter 10; Rosh chapter 3 laws of forbidden foods 36.`,
    '6#א': `Egg of, etc. Like above.`,
    '6#ב': `And some say, etc. Dispute in Tosafot and Ramban; we rule like Tosafot that signs alone do not suffice without naming the species — see there (Lekut).`,
    '7#_': `And some say. See Shach.`,
    '8#_': `And egg of pure, etc. — see there.`,
  },
  'kaf-hachayim': {
    '1#_': `(1) [Seif 1] Signs of eggs to recognize they are from a pure fowl, etc. Shach s.k. 1. Peri Chadash section 1. Rabbenu Tam section 2. Minchat Yehudah section 3. Shafan Dam section 1. Machberet section 1. Zivchei Tzedek section 1.`,
    '2#_': `(2) And witnesses may come and swear. They are false witnesses who wish to cause prohibition. Arukh HaShulchan there. Even a hundred witnesses that it is pure do not help — he certainly lies.`,
    '3#_': `(3) However, where we have a pure fowl eaten by tradition and its signs, and it laid an egg before us in this manner, it appears permitted. Peri Chadash there. Zivchei Tzedek section 2.`,
    '4#_': `(4) An egg whose both ends are rounded or pointed was found in a chicken coop; great concern; they measured and found one end pointed finer than a hair — I said all measures of the Sages are so. Shulchan HaLeket ch. 1 siman 185. Shafan Dam ch. 1.`,
    '5#_': `(5) If one end is round and the other pointed, and white outside and yolk inside, etc. Peri Chadash end of section 5. Likutei Halachot. Kereti section 1. Chochmat Adam 36:11. Beit Yitzchak. Mekor Chaim section 4. Arukh HaShulchan section 15. Only after peeling by hand or utensil — Bach, Knesset HaGedolah, Zivchei Tzedek section 4.`,
    '6#_': `(6) Whoever has scales has a fin — nature testifies; Spanish swordfish with scales but no fin does not contradict Chazal — minority; no need to distinguish sea creature from fish — Peri Chadach section 1.`,
    '7#_': `(7) A small egg found inside a larger egg, all white without yolk — that egg is impure. Maharik in hagahot on Shulchan Aruch; Peri Chadash there; Likutei Halachot; Kereti; Chayei Adam 36:11; Beit Yitzchak; Mekor Chaim section 4; Arukh HaShulchan section 15; only after peeling by hand or utensil.`,
    '8#_': `(8) There. Even an Israelite not established in kashrut we rely when he names a recognized pure species. Shach s.k. 2. Peri Chadash section 2. Avodat HaTaharah section 3. Hagahot Rabbi Akiva Eiger. Beit Yehudah ch. 1 section 8. Kereti section 2; and even without signs when eggs resemble known pure eggs — see there.`,
    '9#_': `(9) If he says from such-and-such fowl, etc. Shach s.k. 3. Kereti section 2.`,
    '10#_': `(10) If he says from a pure fowl and did not state its name, etc. Tur s.k. 2. Peri Chadash section 3. Knesset HaGedolah in hagahot Tur section 1. Kereti section 2; Shulchan Petach David section 2.`,
  },
  'kereti': {
    '1#א': `And even if he says, etc. Even one established in kashrut who says they are from such-and-such fowl and we recognize that fowl is pure — he is not believed, for it is a clear lie; and I wrote in Peleti that there is no contradiction to the Torah — see there.`,
    '1#ב': `Therefore one asks the Israelite hunter. This is Rambam's view: an Israelite suspects he will lie; a non-Jew does not fear — therefore we do not buy from him except by recognizable familiarity; Rama emended "or non-Jew" following dissenting poskim who rely even on a non-Jew when he names a recognized pure species. See Peleti where I reconciled Rambam with the Gemara — see there.`,
    '1#ג': `And we recognize. For otherwise he might err and think it is pure when it is not.`,
    '1#ד': `And he did not state its name. Not specifically when he says its name but we do not know the fowl is pure — only as a general statement.`,
    '1#ה': `Recognizable familiarity. Rambam's view: with a non-Jew this helps; dissenting poskim hold the same for naming a recognized species.`,
    '1#ו': `Eggs of tereifah. We follow the majority; most eggs are kosher.`,
    '2#_': `And the like. If the egg was completed before the chicken became tereifah, it is nevertheless forbidden — see Peleti.`,
    '3#א': `And even if mixed. Mechaber follows Tur that an egg is not nullified in a mixture — see Peleti for discussion.`,
    '3#ב': `Without a mixture. A doubt of nevelah is rabbinic and lenient; a doubt of tereifah is d'oraisa and stringent.`,
    '4#_': `Provided not entangled in sinews. See below siman 87 what I wrote in Peleti.`,
    '5#א': `In its shell. See Peleti for extensive discussion of shells, Yerushalmi, and sixty-one.`,
    '5#ב': `If peeled. See Peleti — dispute whether forbidden peeled with permitted unpeeled; majority of Yerushalmi sources require sixty-one when forbidden is peeled.`,
    '5#ג': `Sixty-one. See Peleti on Rambam vs. poskim on measure; Maharshal and Ran to be stringent for both reasons.`,
    '5#ד': `Or blood. See above siman 66.`,
    '5#ה': `Shells combine. See above siman 99.`,
    '6#_': `And some say. See Peleti for reconciliation with Gemara permitting chick from nevelah egg.`,
    '10#א': `How should he act. Taz: if yolk and white remain visible, signs of such-and-such fowl may help; selling cracked to a non-Jew is permitted per Rashba when signs are known.`,
    '10#ב': `And some say. Where bread of gentiles is permitted, selling tereifah to a non-Jew is permitted per many poskim; custom varies — see Peleti and Semak.`,
    '10#ג': `And so is practiced. Nevertheless do not buy cracked eggs from a non-Jew — see a sage in general rule 67.`,
  },
  'nekudot-hakesef': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 86 Taz s.k. 5) And this is not correct. It does not appear so from Orach Chaim as I will write below; nevertheless some say: bread kneaded with eggs — there the reason is because they rely on the majority; but here, since she broke it, it is like finding it cracked in his hand — we say in end of seif 10 that it is forbidden since there is concern of tereifah that an Israelite sold it to him; and the same here when she broke it — we are concerned lest it was an impure egg; and it appears Orach Chaim and Rama in the hagahah here do not intend this except to exclude eggs of chicken and goose that are more complete when whole — and investigate.`,
    '2#_': `(There s.k. 10) But certainly shells combine and the sixty-one includes shells — as Shach wrote in small seif 14.`,
    '3#_': `(There s.k. 12) This does not resolve anything — they certainly added one to the measure, but shells are good.`,
    '4#_': `(There s.k. 14) Some say this applies only to terumah where the Sages decreed extra stringency because terumah is common; and specifically for terumah they decreed lest impure terumah be kept until sowing time; and here, since there is only one egg, they did not decree — as with safek tereifah where we wait until it lays.`,
    '5#_': `(Hagahah) See Beit Yosef daf 77 side 4 — Rashba wondered why Rif omitted disgusting eggs from the baraita; it appears Rif explains Rashi: disgusting eggs are impure eggs cooked with pure — the pure are permitted as mere moisture and do not impart taste to others; as in Yerushalmi Terumot per Rashi; poskim bring this; Rif states this explicitly in chapter Gid HaNasheh — investigate.`,
  },
  'peleti': {
    '1#_': `And even if the seller says, etc. See Shach s.k. 1 — even an Israelite established in kashrut is not believed as a clear lie; so too Peri Chadash; and as I wrote in siman 83 s.k. 4 — in one established in kashrut investigation is required, but if he withstands investigation he is believed; see there.`,
    '2#_': `Therefore one asks the Israelite hunter, etc. This is Rambam's view: only an Israelite suspect fears; a non-Jew does not — therefore we do not buy except by recognizable familiarity. Rama follows dissenting poskim. See Shach; Rambam's text read "from anyone" not "from a non-Jew." Peleti reconciles Abaye's challenge and the baraita — see there for full discussion.`,
    '3#_': `Until he sees the signs. Signs alone suffice without naming the species per Rama; see Peleti.`,
    '4#_': `Egg of nevelah and tereifah. Shach s.k. 10 holds tereifah egg is d'oraisa even if not yet tereifah at laying; Rashba holds completed before prohibition — Beit Yosef ruled like Rashba for doubt to be lenient on nevelah; Shach disagrees — see Peleti supporting Rashba from Beitzah and Ramban.`,
    '5#_': `And even if mixed in a thousand. Shach questions Mechaber following Tur on davar shebeminyan; Peleti argues egg nullifies one in two per Rambam — see there.`,
    '6#_': `If in its shell. Extensive discussion in Peleti on Yerushalmi, Tosafot, shells, and Mechaber vs. Yerushalmi — see there.`,
    '7#_': `Sixty-one. Peleti on Rambam vs. Tosafot on measure — see there.`,
    '8#_': `That the fowl began to form. Shach challenged Perishah; Bach wrote fabricated words; Peleti reconciles Tur, Rambam, and Maharach in Pesachim that blood near formation is like dust d'oraisa — see there.`,
    '9#_': `We wait with it. Taz and Rambam differ on safek tereifah laying; Peleti on twenty-one days and siman 57 — see there.`,
    '10#_': `How should he act. Selling cracked egg to non-Jew — dispute Tosafot, Rashba, Semak, custom; Peleti on bread kneaded with eggs — see there.`,
  },
  'pitchei-teshuva': {
    '1#_': `Egg of nevelah. Avodat HaTaharah; see Pri Megadim who questions whether complete eggs from those nine nevelot listed in Pesachim chapter 1 are d'oraisa — investigate; see Likutei Halachot letter 14.`,
    '2#_': `And it was found tereifah. See responsum Chinuch Beit Yosef siman 86 — complex case; see there.`,
    '3#_': `And shells of prohibition. See below siman 99 s.k. 1 in name of responsum Maharit siman 27.`,
    '4#_': `And l'chatchila one should be careful. Avodat HaTaharah s.k. 14; see She'eilot Ya'avetz ch. 1 siman 161 who rejects Taz's reason; Orach Chaim letter 14.`,
    '5#_': `Whole eggs. Responsum Beit Avraham ch. 1 end siman 86 — whether "whole" means literally whole days or whole eggs; requires study.`,
    '6#_': `Egg of, etc. Responsum Nodah BeYehudah Tinyana ch. 2 end siman 186 — baraita Chullin 64 not only tereifah egg; see there and Orach Chaim ch. 1 end siman 70.`,
    '7#_': `In a bowl and sell it. See responsum Chasam Sofer siman 19.`,
  },
  'rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 86 Shach s.k. 10) Not require formation of a soul. It is astonishing to say an egg of tereifah and nevelah is a complete creature — how is fowl nevelah different from egg of nevelah?`,
  },
  'siftei-kohen': {
    '1#א': `["And even if the seller says, etc. Explanation: Even if the seller says — even an Israelite established in kashrut that they are from such-and-such fowl and we recognize that that fowl is pure — we do not rely on him, for he is certainly impure; and even if he shows us the fowl and says this is the fowl from which the eggs come, he is not believed, for he certainly lies. And see above siman 83 s.k. 26."]`,
    '1#ב': `["Therefore one asks the Israelite hunter, etc. Mechaber's view is like Rambam chapter 3 of Forbidden Foods — specifically with an Israelite who is not established in kashrut we rely on him, but with a non-Jew we never rely on him; therefore he wrote that one does not buy eggs from a non-Jew unless he recognized them and has recognizable familiarity with them; and as Rambam wrote there, meaning specifically in such a case we buy from him, but when he says from such-and-such fowl and it is pure we do not rely on him. But Ramban, Ran, and Tur disagree and hold that with a non-Jew too we rely on him as with an Israelite who is not established in kashrut; and so too in Sifrei — this is the view of many poskim from what they wrote regarding fish innards in seif 2 of Avodah Zarah; and so I proved there from the Gemara in Pesachim that this is primary. I also proved there from the Gemara in chapter Kol HaBasar (Chullin daf 116) — see there; and this is Rama's view. And what he did not emend at all from what Mechaber wrote "therefore one does not buy," etc., is because he relied on what he wrote before — or non-Jew — meaning that with a non-Jew too we rely; and automatically it appears that Mechaber did not write this except according to his view; and Taz mixed up the matter by first writing "or non-Jew" and afterward writing "therefore one does not buy," etc. And see in siman 83 s.k. 24 and 25."]`,
    '1#ג': `["That it is from such-and-such fowl, etc. The reason: when he says it is from such-and-such fowl it is a presumption he does not lie because he fears lest they bring eggs of that fowl and they see they do not resemble these and he is shamed; unlike when he only says it is from a pure fowl — when they bring eggs of a pure fowl and they do not resemble them, he can say: I did not tell you these are from this fowl but from another fowl you do not recognize, and it is pure."]`,
    '1#ד': `["Unless he recognized them, etc. And according to Rama in his hagahah — even without recognizable familiarity we rely on him when he says it is from such-and-such fowl and we recognize that it is pure — as I wrote in s.k. 2."]`,
    '1#ה': `["And we are not concerned for them, etc. For most eggs are kosher."]`,
    '2#א': `["Now they practice buying eggs indiscriminately, etc. Because impure fowl are not found among us — it appears even without recognizable familiarity, and he does not say they are from such-and-such fowl and it is pure, and even if he says nothing at all they buy even from a non-Jew, provided both ends are not rounded or pointed, because impure fowl are not found among us. But the Rav the Maggid explained that what they practice buying now from anyone is because they buy only chicken and goose eggs, and those are recognizable by familiarity — end of his words. It appears specifically when he has recognizable familiarity with them it is permitted; and such is Rama's view in his hagahah; and so too in a sage at the beginning of general rule 66 explicitly; and it appears from Rama's words in that sage that for chicken and goose eggs one need not check at all or look for other signs; and so it appears from his words here in the hagahah; and so wrote in Orach Chaim general rule 42 seif 7; and see end of this seif and what he wrote there in s.k. 4."]`,
    '2#ב': `["Or geese. And it is written in that sage there, and so is the custom to buy bar-goose eggs (called enten), some in white shells and some in green shells, because they recognize in them that they are bar-goose eggs whose way is thus — end of his words. And this is what he wrote here "and the like."]`,
    '3#א': `["An egg of nevelah and tereifah, etc. An egg of nevelah is forbidden only d'rabbanan, for from the Torah it is permitted, for it is impossible it should be completed after death; but an egg of tereifah is forbidden from the Torah because afterward it was completed in prohibition, and even if it did not become tereifah except near shechitah it is forbidden from the Torah (they do not distinguish) — so too Beit Yosef, and the Rav agreed with him in a sage at the beginning of general rule 81; and see above end of seif 99 and what he wrote there, and above siman 87 seif 5, and what he wrote there."]`,
    '3#ב': `["And even if it was mixed in, etc. This applies also to an egg of nevelah, for since it is an egg of nevelah the prohibition is certainly established and it is not nullified; unlike when it is a doubt of an egg of nevelah, which is a rabbinic doubt and lenient."]`,
    '3#ג': `["In a thousand all are forbidden. It is astonishing — this is only according to Beit HaTaharah, who holds that an item sold sometimes by estimate and sometimes by count, like an egg, is not nullified; but according to what Mechaber ruled below siman 110 as Rambam and his supporters — that even items always sold by count are nullified; and even Rama in his hagahah ruled there that an item always sold by count is not nullified — nevertheless eggs that are sometimes sold in a basket full of eggs are nullified, as stated in the Gemara at the beginning of Beitzah explicitly, and as will be explained there (and so too in a sage at the end of general rule 63 explicitly). If so, here why in a thousand are all forbidden? It should nullify in a majority — dry in dry, one in two is nullified, as below siman 109; and even though Tur also wrote that in a thousand they are forbidden, the reason is Tur's view that an item sometimes sold by count and sometimes by estimate, like an egg, is not nullified, as Beit HaEzer brought in name of Baal HaItur in siman 110; and even though he brought Rambam's view there last, nevertheless it is possible he holds Baal HaItur's view is primary to be stringent; and so too in a sage at the end of seif 41 of general rule 42 that Tur's view is like Baal HaItur's view. Or Tur follows Tosafot and Rosh that an egg is considered a complete creature and we do not require formation of a soul; but Mechaber ruled in siman 110 that specifically an egg that has a chick in it is a complete creature since it has a soul, but an ordinary egg is not, since there was no life in it — if so, why if mixed in a thousand are all forbidden? Requires study; and for practical law see above siman 110 s.k. 9."]`,
    '3#ד': `["Or if it is an egg of a slaughtered animal, etc. It appears to me that it is not necessary to discuss a doubt whether this egg is from a nevelah chicken or from another chicken that was slaughtered — it is permitted; but even if we know certainly from which chicken it is, only that there is doubt whether the chicken is nevelah or slaughtered — such as doubt in shechitah where the halachah is that it is invalid — the egg is permitted. Investigate."]`,
    '3#ה': `["And see above siman 81. See there s.k. 2 — it was explained correctly in s.k. 4."]`,
    '4#_': `Provided it is not entangled in sinews. But if it was entangled in sinews, behold this is like meat separating from a living animal and it is forbidden — end of his words Tur in name of Rashba; and see above siman 87 s.k. 9 what he wrote on this.`,
    '5#א': `["And if it is peeled, etc. Rambam's and Ramban's view (and so R'ah in Beit HaBachur daf 108 side 2) is that there is no distinction; and poskim wrote that this is how our Gemara implies, except that one should be concerned for the Yerushalmi; and in my humble opinion Rambam and his supporters — in the Yerushalmi it appears they do not distinguish in this way but distinguish that when not peeled one does not need sixty, but forty or fifty-one suffice because shells raise the prohibition; but when peeled a greater measure is required — and this speaks of an egg with a chick in it — see there and investigate."]`,
    '5#ב': `["If it is peeled it forbids. It appears that even though the others are also in their shells it is forbidden until sixty-one; and so explained Perishah seif 11, and Bach in his last comment wrote a great astonishment that he would write in a book a fabricated explanation that has no root or branch in the Gemara or in rulings of the Geonim; rather the matter is clear that when the others are not peeled, even if the forbidden one is peeled, it does not forbid — end of his words. And I am greatly astonished at him, for in the Yerushalmi from which poskim brought this law it is stated explicitly thus, and so too Rashba in Pesachim, Semag negative commandment 132 (daf 45 side 1), Semak siman 227, and Rashba in responsum siman 46, whose language is: And as we read in the Yerushalmi, etc. — peeled with not-peeled require a different measure — it teaches that whenever the forbidden egg is peeled it forbids the permitted ones even if the permitted ones are not peeled; the shell does not prevent absorption — end of his words. And in truth what they brought for this is what he wrote in his composition, whose language is: It appears one must explain that all the eggs are peeled; for if the others are not peeled, sixty-one would not be required because there are large and small eggs — for certainly the shell on sixty eggs fills what is missing from the small ones — end of his words. And this means certainly shells combine to nullify the prohibition, as the Rav wrote in his hagahah nearby; but we say thus: when you count shells to nullify, you need so many eggs that among them all there are sixty-one; and by way of example: if there are fifty permitted eggs and the shells hold ten eggs, that is insufficient (and it appears to my humble opinion from Chullin 98 side 1, where it says there one who came before R' Gamliel said: Father, I estimated in forty-one and I in forty-eight, and Rashi explained there the sugya — it appears he speaks of eggs discussed there; and he did not go to the end of his view that the wording of the baraita does not imply this generally, but it appears he speaks of eggs; and R' Chiya challenged there: Is there any forty in a seah? and the Gemara answers hyperbole — meaning certainly it is impossible that forty large eggs would amount to sixty-one; but according to Rashi's explanation there is room to challenge: why did he challenge? And why answer hyperbole? For if we estimate in half a seah it is lenient, and even in forty it suffices, as Mar bar Ashi said there who estimates in forty half-olives — and so is stated explicitly in Yerushalmi Terumot chapter Bitzel as explained — and this is clear) and not like Perishah seif 12 who resolved this that sixty in the combination of shells does not suffice because the Sages were concerned lest a forbidden egg be so large that its interior would be as large as other permitted eggs with their shells — end of his words; for this is a matter that has no dawn, and sense and perception contradict it — how is it possible that in shells of sixty eggs there would not be as much as one egg? Rather certainly the main point is as I wrote, and it is clear and true."]`,
    '5#ג': `["And one needs sixty-one permitted eggs, etc. The reason: many poskim wrote that because there are large and small eggs, and one who comes to nullify an egg does not pay attention to see if this one and those are exactly equal — therefore they added one — end of his words. And for this reason when it was cooked in a permitted dish one needs only sixty of the dish to nullify it — this is Mechaber's view; but Rambam in chapter 4 of Forbidden Foods law 19 wrote the reason is because it is a complete creature in itself they made a distinction and added to its measure — end of his words (and all this speaks only of nullifying its emission, not the body of the egg, as Rashba and Beit Yosef state, and it is plain). And I proved in my book that Rambam means the egg itself is a complete creature in itself, as Tosafot, Rosh, and Rashba in Pesachim in name of Raavad, and Beit Yosef brings this below siman 110 and other poskim — that an egg is a complete creature; and Rashba in his novellae daf 106 side 1, and Beit Yosef understood that Rambam calls a chick a complete creature (and therefore for us, who rule that peeled or pierced forbids even without a chick in it, one needs sixty-one even in a dish, to be stringent). And according to Rambam's reason, even in a dish one needs sixty-one of the dish to nullify it; however according to Rambam's reason, if it became tereifah in a bowl one needs only sixty; and according to poskim and Mechaber one needs sixty-one; and the Rav also wrote in his hagahah; and I wrote there that Rambam's reason appears primary in the Gemara, and so it appears in Tosafot Pesachim (daf 98 side 1) s.v. "The question is," etc. However, it is difficult: why did they write there and nevertheless they resolved with difficulty and did not resolve that certainly all Torah prohibitions are nullified in sixty, and also in eggs sixty in a dish suffices, except that it was cooked with eggs — this case is different because one who comes to measure does not pay attention — they added one to the measure so there will certainly be sixty; but certainly they hold the reason is not because there are large and small eggs, etc., but from the law one needs sixty-one — see there and investigate. Therefore one should be stringent for both reasons; and such is Maharshal in Pesachim siman 43 and Ran — to be stringent for both reasons; and see in siman 106 more laws of cooking eggs."]`,
    '5#ד': `["And an egg that has a chick or blood, etc. Because the meat imparts taste — and there is no difference between impure and pure since there is a chick in it; and the same applies to blood found in it — a drop of blood that spreads through all of it, as above siman 66, and it is considered as if there is a chick in it — so too poskim; and specifically when it is known the blood is in the place that forbids it; but in a doubt of mixtures it is permitted as above siman 66 and s.k. 4 in the hagahah, and as I wrote there, and above siman 98 s.k. 7."]`,
    '5#ה': `["And see above siman 99. Meaning: there the sixty-one in the hagahah was explained — where there is no great loss one may not combine bones of prohibition to nullify — and the same applies here; and as I wrote there s.k. 1."]`,
    '6#_': `["And some say, etc. And below siman 98 Mechaber wrote: an egg that has a chick in it or a drop of blood that was cooked with others needs sixty-one to nullify its emission — end of his words; and it appears that an egg of impure fowl or of tereifah does not need nullification, and Rama did not emend there at all, because it appears primary in the Gemara and poskim like Mechaber."]`,
    '7#א': `["A chick born from an egg of nevelah and tereifah — permitted. The reason is stated in the Gemara and poskim: because the chick is not formed from the egg until after it spoils, and it is like mere dust."]`,
    '7#ב': `["And some say, etc. And Taz omitted this entire hagahah — and rightly intended: why should we be stringent in something found explicitly permitted in the Gemara and all poskim; also in Darkei Moshe and in a sage general rule 64 seif 2 he wrote on Orach Chaim that there is no reason for his words; and so in signs of a sage there, plain like Mechaber."]`,
    '7#ג': `["And l'chatchila one should be careful. Explanation: with this chick that was born one should be careful l'chatchila."]`,
    '8#_': `["That the fowl began to form in it, etc. So too Tur in name of Rambam; and Perishah challenged: for Tur wrote above siman 66 that if it is known the chick is formed one is liable on it from the Torah; and he resolved: there, blood is found inside the egg and from it the chick is born — therefore forbidden from the Torah because of blood; unlike here where the blood already turned and became clotted and is dust and has no d'oraisa prohibition — end of his words. And Bach in a later pamphlet wrote on this that these are fabricated words without support or proof, and resolved in his composition that Tur holds like R' Yehudah who brought in the conclusion in siman 66 that he permits even blood if found outside the knot; if so for us no egg blood is forbidden d'oraisa, and Mechaber's words cannot be reconciled at all — for in siman 66 he wrote one is liable on it d'oraisa, and here he wrote a blow of rebelliousness — end of his words. But in truth, according to what Beit Yosef wrote in siman 66, Rambam (here) disagrees with what Tur wrote — if it is known the chick is formed one is liable d'oraisa — if so Mechaber's words in Shulchan Aruch cannot be reconciled; but one may say that Tur himself brought Rambam's words in general, and on Bach's challenge: for Tur wrote in siman 66 in general one is liable from the Torah, and Rosh in a responsum (Beit Yosef brings this in siman 66) holds that formed blood is forbidden d'oraisa; and what Tur wrote afterward that R' Yehudah permits blood even outside the knot does not contradict what he wrote first in general that one is liable d'oraisa — for certainly when found outside the knot it is entirely permitted, and when found on the knot, if it is formed blood, it is forbidden from the Torah; and moreover from what they wrote, Rosh and Tur afterward in siman 66 "and good to be stringent," etc. — see there, it appears explicitly that he holds formed blood is d'oraisa; only Perishah's words appear clear in their reason, and I found support and proof for his words from what Maharach in Pesachim wrote — that Ra'avyah received from Ra'avan: if blood is in the yolk in a place where the start of the chick is not recognizable, then only it is forbidden because of the law of blood; but if full of blood and the place where the chick begins is recognizable, it is permitted, for near the formation it is like mere dust — end of his words. And even though Beit Yosef wrote in siman 66 on this, and it appears these words are rejected and it is not the view of poskim — end of his words — one may say they disagree only on permitting entirely, but from the Torah it is nevertheless permitted; and Mechaber's words may also be reconciled thus, and he retracted from what he wrote in siman 66 that Rambam disagrees with Tur; and so it appears somewhat in his book Bedek HaBayit in this siman."]`,
    '9#א': `["A fowl that is a safek tereifah, etc. But certainly tereifah — no; and see siman 81 s.k. 5; and these laws were already explained in end of seif 57 — see there."]`,
    '9#ב': `["If it will lay again, etc. Meaning: if it will lay again and lay eggs — but laying alone is not permitted, as above siman 57 s.k. 45."]`,
    '9#ג': `["Kosher. And likewise all eggs laid previously are kosher."]`,
    '10#א': `["One does not sell. See above siman 84 s.k. 18."]`,
    '10#ב': `["In what case is this said. Meaning: in what case is it forbidden to buy from a non-Jew when we see it cracked in his hand."]`,
    '10#ג': `["Bread kneaded with eggs in a place, etc. See above siman 112 and what he wrote there."]`,
    '10#ד': `["And some say, etc. Tur's view appears that even where they are not careful about bread of gentiles, it is permitted to sell him a tereifah egg; and so it appears to me that this is Tosafot in chapter Eizehu Mekoman (daf 64 side 1 s.v. signs), Rashba, Mordechai there, and Ran there explicitly; and so appears Semag; and so from other poskim. The reason for all of them: we are not concerned lest he mix it with bread and sell it to an Israelite; also most of their dough is not kneaded with eggs but with whole eggs, as stated in Shulchan Aruch siman 98 and in Orach Chaim of Maharil in laws of eggs and other poskim. And what the Rav wrote, and so is practiced — is according to what is written in a sage at the end of general rule 67 seif 4 in name of Orach Chaim general rule 42, that so they practice; but in our Orach Chaim it is not written there that so they practice, and I have not seen such a custom; and so it appears explicitly from Maharshal in Pesachim s.k. 88 that it is permitted. However, it may be that all these poskim speak of a non-Jew who is not signed; but a non-Jew who is commonly signed often mixes with bread — cracked eggs — and we are concerned lest he sell to an Israelite; in this Semak and Orach Chaim are concerned; Bach also wrote this reasoning, even though from the Rav's words in that sage and here it appears forbidden to sell to any non-Jew."]`,
    '10#ה': `["And so is practiced in a place, etc. And it is written in a sage that nevertheless one should not buy cracked eggs from a non-Jew — see there."]`,
  },
  'turei-zahav': {
    '1#א': `One relies on him. For there is a presumption he does not lie, for he fears lest they bring eggs of that fowl and they see they do not resemble these; and there is no room to say that since there are raven eggs resembling dove eggs, as stated in the Gemara, he could evade and say they are dove eggs; nevertheless he fears lest an Israelite set his mind on them that there cannot fail to be some difference between them — Beit Yosef in name of Rashba.`,
    '1#ב': `And he did not state its name. The same applies when he states its name but we do not recognize it. Tur wrote: if he says "from such-and-such fowl," etc., but if he says plainly, etc., and does not mention it — why did he not state the reverse of the first clause — that he states its name but we do not recognize it? It appears Tur's intent is that we require two elevations: first, that he state its name explicitly; second, that it be from a species common among us. When he wrote "and we recognize it," he means the majority of the people know with us — and thereby we gain that the non-Jew fears lest he be caught in a lie; but if it is not common among us, what help is recognition, for the non-Jew thinks the Israelite will not recognize it, and one who wishes to lie will distance his testimony. If there is only one elevation here, not both, it is unnecessary if he states its name and it is not common — plainly he does not fear, as we said; and he need not state its name except when he says in this language that it is from fowl common among us — meaning we recognize the pure species common among us — then the non-Jew fears lest it become clear it is not from those common species and he certainly would not lie; it teaches us: not only when he says "from such-and-such fowl and we recognize it" — for it is common; but if he says plainly "from a pure fowl," meaning from those species common among us, without stating its name explicitly, it is forbidden; and all of this is correct in law and language.`,
    '1#ג': `One does not buy eggs from a non-Jew. Tur wrote: and it appears there is no distinction between a non-Jew and a suspect Israelite. Beit Yosef brought in name of R' Yitzchak ibn Chaviv two explanations, and wrote that according to the law the second explanation is primary, though the wording does not imply it. It appears the second explanation must be that Tur wrote "and it appears" as a comment on Rambam, and Rambam himself does not hold thus — for he wrote nearby that even from a non-Jew recognition helps without recognizable familiarity, but Rambam disagrees on this. Nevertheless it appears possible to prove from the wording, per the second explanation: if per the first, Rambam would say the Gemara speaks of a suspect Israelite, but Rambam distinguishes by reasoning that with a non-Jew one needs recognizable familiarity, and Tur disagrees and says a non-Jew is like a suspect Israelite — he should have said "and it appears there is no distinction between a suspect Israelite and a non-Jew"; if per the second explanation, Rambam speaks of an Israelite in general as in the Gemara, but not of a non-Jew; therefore Tur wrote correctly that it does not appear from Rambam's intent to distinguish specifically a non-Jew — rather even a suspect Israelite is like a non-Jew; therefore he states first what was stated in the comment — a non-Jew — and Rambam intended to innovate that recognizable familiarity is required; on this he said the law is that a suspect Israelite is like a non-Jew.`,
    '1#ד': `Lest they are eggs of tereifah. For we follow the majority.`,
    '2#_': `Meaning chicken eggs. Explanation: we know certainly it was whole and had the form of a chicken egg; but if a non-Jewess brought and broke it before us unintentionally and we had not yet recognized by familiarity that they are chicken eggs, it is forbidden as implied here and in Orach Chaim general rule 42, where R' Yitzchak wrote: and this applies to a chicken egg, but for eggs of other fowl one must examine the signs — end of his words. In truth this is not so, for even bread kneaded with eggs at the end of this siman we say it is permitted; rather in all cases we rely on the majority, except where the non-Jew explicitly says they are from an uncommon species — then signs are required. This is written in a sage in name of Tosafot and Rosh; in name of the Rav the Maggid he also wrote that we buy only chicken and goose eggs; Rama's words here are imprecise.`,
    '3#א': `Even if completed. Beit Yosef in name of Rashba: even if it laid immediately after becoming tereifah it is forbidden — a decree lest it be entangled in sinews.`,
    '3#ב': `And even if mixed in a thousand. Because it was an item counted by number; and it is astonishing on Mechaber — see in siman 110 seif 1 he ruled like Rambam that even items always sold by count are nullified; investigate for practical law.`,
    '3#ג': `Doubt whether an egg of nevelah. An egg of nevelah is forbidden only d'rabbanan; when mixed, the prohibition is established and it is not nullified. An egg of tereifah is forbidden d'oraisa because it was completed in prohibition — therefore they were stringent in doubt.`,
    '5#א': `In its shell it does not forbid. It will not impart taste when in its shell, since there is no substance there — only moisture; unlike an egg with a chick where there is actual prohibition that is a real substance — whatever emerges from it, even via the shell, forbids the others.`,
    '5#ב': `If it is peeled. It appears it was peeled alone and cooked with others that are not peeled; Perishah explained thus; and if the forbidden is peeled and the permitted are not peeled, everything is permitted — end of Perishah; and I am greatly astonished, for in the Yerushalmi from which poskim brought this law it is explicit, and so Rashba in Pesachim, Semag, Semak, and Rashba in a responsum — peeled with not-peeled require a different measure; it teaches that whenever the forbidden egg is peeled it forbids the permitted even if they are not peeled. And in truth what they brought is from his composition: it appears one must explain that all eggs are peeled; if the others are not peeled, sixty-one would not be needed because of large and small eggs — for certainly the shell of sixty eggs fills what is missing from the small. And this means shells certainly combine to nullify the prohibition, as the Rav wrote in his hagahah nearby; but we say: when counting shells to nullify, one needs enough eggs that in total there are sixty-one; by way of example: fifty permitted eggs whose shells hold ten eggs is insufficient (and it appears from Chullin 98 side 1 that this is implied). Not like Perishah seif 12. This has no dawn; sense and perception contradict that shells of sixty eggs would not equal one egg — rather the main point is as I wrote, and it is clear and true.`,
    '5#ג': `If pierced. See seif 95 seif 2 what I challenged on this.`,
    '5#ד': `And an egg that has a chick. If cooked alone with permitted eggs, sixty-one permitted eggs are required even if the egg with the chick is not peeled; all the more so in a dish it forbids up to sixty of the dish, and shells of permission combine as the Rav wrote in his hagahah nearby; but we say: when counting shells to nullify, one needs so many eggs that in total there are sixty-one; by example: fifty permitted eggs whose shells hold ten eggs is insufficient.`,
    '5#ה': `Or blood. Because the meat imparts taste — and there is no difference between impure and pure since there is a chick in it; and the same applies to a drop of blood that spreads through all of it, as above siman 66; it is considered as if there is a chick in it; so too poskim; specifically when the blood is known to be in the forbidding place; in a doubt of mixtures it is permitted as above siman 66 and s.k. 4 in the hagahah, and as I wrote there, and above siman 98 s.k. 7.`,
    '7#_': `And some say. See Shach.`,
    '8#_': `That the fowl began to form. See what I wrote in siman 66 seif 2.`,
    '9#_': `We wait with it. And see siman 57.`,
  },
  'yad-avraham': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 86 seif 10) Chick born from egg of nevelah and tereifah permitted. Because the chick is not formed until after the egg spoils and is like dust — Taz and Shach. Investigate Yerushalmi Sotah regarding egg of hekdesh that became a chick — see there.`,
  },
};
